Sending keys: ['cat << \'EOF\' > generate_answers.py\nimport json\n\nset_id = "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a"\n\nanswers = [\n    {\n        "qid": "b2531657ba5c7a78",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "Paxlovid (nirmatrelvir co-packaged with ritonavir) has significant drug interactions because ritonavir is a strong CYP3A inhibitor. Co-administration of Paxlovid with drugs primarily metabolized by CYP3A can increase plasma concentrations of these medications, potentially leading to severe, life-threatening, or fatal events. Conversely, co-administration with strong CYP3A inducers can significantly decrease nirmatrelvir and ritonavir concentrations, leading to a loss of therapeutic effect and potential viral resistance. Clinically serious interactions are commonly reported with calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine) and calcium channel blockers.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0013",\n                "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "Initiation of PAXLOVID, which contains ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A or initiation of medications metabolized by CYP3A in patients already receiving PAXLOVID, may increase plasma concentrations of medications metabolized by CYP3A. Medications that induce CYP3A may decrease concentrations of PAXLOVID."\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "interacting_mechanism": "Ritonavir is a strong CYP3A inhibitor",\n            "most_common_serious_interacting_medications": [\n                "calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine)",\n                "calcium channel blockers"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "6f12607a1ba14dd5",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "Medications that should be avoided (or are contraindicated) with Paxlovid include: alpha 1-adrenoreceptor antagonists (alfuzosin); antianginals (ranolazine); antiarrhythmics (amiodarone, dronedarone, flecainide, propafenone, quinidine); anti-gout agents (colchicine in patients with renal and/or hepatic impairment); antipsychotics (lurasidone, pimozide); benign prostatic hyperplasia agents (silodosin, tamsulosin); cardiovascular agents (eplerenone, ivabradine, aliskiren, ticagrelor, vorapaxar, clopidogrel); ergot derivatives (dihydroergotamine, ergotamine, methylergonovine); HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (lovastatin, simvastatin); immunosuppressants (voclosporin, everolimus, sirolimus, cyclosporine/tacrolimus when monitoring is not feasible); microsomal triglyceride transfer protein inhibitors (lomitapide); migraine medications (eletriptan, ubrogepant, rimegepant); mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists (finerenone); opioid antagonists (naloxegol); PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil for PAH, tadalafil for pulmonary hypertension); sedative/hypnotics (triazolam, oral midazolam, suvorexant); serotonin receptor 1A agonist/serotonin receptor 2A antagonists (flibanserin); vasopressin receptor antagonists (tolvaptan); and strong CYP3A inducers (apalutamide, enzalutamide, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, phenytoin, rifampin, rifapentine, lumacaftor/ivacaftor, St. John\'s Wort). Concomitant use of anticancer drugs (encorafenib, ivosidenib, neratinib, venetoclax, ibrutinib), anticoagulants (rivaroxaban, dabigatran), antifungals (voriconazole), antivirals (glecaprevir/pibrentasvir), and bronchodilators (salmeterol) should also be avoided.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0012",\n                "section": "4 CONTRAINDICATIONS",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is contraindicated with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions and drugs that are strong CYP3A inducers where significantly reduced nirmatrelvir or ritonavir plasma concentrations may be associated with the potential for loss of virologic response and possible resistance."\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "contraindicated_medications": [\n                "alfuzosin", "ranolazine", "amiodarone", "dronedarone", "flecainide", "propafenone", "quinidine",\n                "colchicine", "lurasidone", "pimozide", "silodosin", "eplerenone", "ivabradine",\n                "dihydroergotamine", "ergotamine", "methylergonovine", "lovastatin", "simvastatin",\n                "voclosporin", "lomitapide", "eletriptan", "ubrogepant", "finerenone", "naloxegol",\n                "sildenafil", "triazolam", "oral midazolam", "flibanserin", "tolvaptan"\n            ],\n            "strong_cyp3a_inducers": [\n                "apalutamide", "enzalutamide", "carbamazepine", "phenobarbital", "primidone", "phenytoin",\n                "rifampin", "rifapentine", "lumacaftor/ivacaftor", "St. John\'s Wort"\n            ],\n            "other_medications_to_avoid": [\n                "tamsulosin", "encorafenib", "ivosidenib", "neratinib", "venetoclax", "ibrutinib",\n                "rivaroxaban", "dabigatran", "voriconazole", "aliskiren", "ticagrelor", "vorapaxar",\n                "clopidogrel", "glecaprevir/pibrentasvir", "everolimus", "sirolimus", "salmeterol",\n                "rimegepant", "suvorexant", "tadalafil"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "fbab4114f66e2dda",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "The important warnings and precautions associated with Paxlovid are: 1) Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions: Ritonavir is a strong CYP3A inhibitor, and co-administration with CYP3A-metabolized drugs can lead to serious, life-threatening, or fatal events, while strong CYP3A inducers can decrease Paxlovid exposure, causing loss of efficacy and resistance. 2) Hypersensitivity Reactions: Anaphylaxis, serious skin reactions (including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome), and other hypersensitivity reactions have been reported. 3) Hepatotoxicity: Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir. 4) Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development: Ritonavir co-administration may lead to HIV-1 developing resistance to HIV protease inhibitors in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0013",\n                "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "Severe, life-threatening, and/or fatal adverse reactions due to drug interactions have been reported in patients treated with PAXLOVID."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0014",\n                "section": "5.2 Hypersensitivity Reactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "Anaphylaxis, serious skin reactions (including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome), and other hypersensitivity reactions have been reported with PAXLOVID ."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0015",\n                "section": "5.3 Hepatotoxicity",\n                "supporting_quote": "Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0016",\n                "section": "5.4 Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development",\n                "supporting_quote": "Because nirmatrelvir is co-administered with ritonavir, there may be a risk of HIV-1 developing resistance to HIV protease inhibitors in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection ."\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "warnings_and_precautions": [\n                "Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "Hypersensitivity Reactions",\n                "Hepatotoxicity",\n                "Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "2d63e0a82215867f",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "The safety risks listed for Paxlovid include: 1) Most common adverse reactions (incidence \\u22651% and greater than placebo) from clinical trials: dysgeusia (5% vs. <1%) and diarrhea (3% vs. 2%). 2) Adverse reactions identified during Emergency Use Authorization (EUA) use: anaphylaxis, hypersensitivity reactions, toxic epidermal necrolysis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, headache, hypertension, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and malaise. 3) Serious warnings/precautions: risk of serious adverse reactions due to drug interactions (especially with calcineurin inhibitors and calcium channel blockers), hepatotoxicity (transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, jaundice), and risk of HIV-1 resistance development in patients with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0018",\n                "section": "6.1 Clinical Trials Experience",\n                "supporting_quote": "The most common adverse reactions (\\u22651% incidence in the PAXLOVID group and occurring at a greater frequency than in the placebo group) were dysgeusia (5% and <1%, respectively) and diarrhea (3% and 2%, respectively)."\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "most_common_adverse_reactions": {\n                "dysgeusia": "5%",\n                "diarrhea": "3%"\n            },\n            "eua_adverse_reactions": [\n                "anaphylaxis", "hypersensitivity reactions", "toxic epidermal necrolysis",\n                "Stevens-Johnson syndrome", "headache", "hypertension", "abdominal pain",\n                "nausea", "vomiting", "malaise"\n            ],\n            "serious_risks": [\n                "Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "Hypersensitivity Reactions",\n                "Hepatotoxicity",\n                "Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "cf74a5a8ac9b19de",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "Paxlovid is contraindicated in: 1) Patients with a history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., toxic epidermal necrolysis [TEN] or Stevens-Johnson syndrome) to its active ingredients (nirmatrelvir or ritonavir) or any other components of the product. 2) Co-administration with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions (e.g., alfuzosin, ranolazine, amiodarone, dronedarone, flecainide, propafenone, quinidine, colchicine in patients with renal and/or hepatic impairment, lurasidone, pimozide, silodosin, eplerenone, ivabradine, dihydroergotamine, ergotamine, methylergonovine, lovastatin, simvastatin, voclosporin, lomitapide, eletriptan, ubrogepant, finerenone, naloxegol, sildenafil for PAH, triazolam, oral midazolam, flibanserin, tolvaptan). 3) Co-administration with drugs that are strong CYP3A inducers where significantly reduced nirmatrelvir or ritonavir plasma concentrations may be associated with potential loss of virologic response and possible resistance (e.g., apalutamide, enzalutamide, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, phenytoin, rifampin, rifapentine, lumacaftor/ivacaftor, St. John\'s Wort).",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0012",\n                "section": "4 CONTRAINDICATIONS",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is contraindicated in patients with a history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions e.g., toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) or Stevens-Johnson syndrome] to its active ingredients (nirmatrelvir or ritonavir) or any other components of the product."\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "contraindications": [\n                "History of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions to active ingredients or components",\n                "Co-administration with drugs primarily metabolized by CYP3A with serious/life-threatening risks",\n                "Co-administration with strong CYP3A inducers with loss of virologic response risk"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "c4dc176c4827f0ef",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "Paxlovid should not be taken by: 1) Patients with a history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., toxic epidermal necrolysis or Stevens-Johnson syndrome) to nirmatrelvir, ritonavir, or any other components of Paxlovid. 2) Patients taking any contraindicated medications, including those primarily metabolized by CYP3A for which elevated concentrations cause serious/life-threatening reactions, or strong CYP3A inducers. 3) Patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class C), as Paxlovid is not recommended for use in this population due to a lack of pharmacokinetic or safety data.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0012",\n                "section": "4 CONTRAINDICATIONS",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is contraindicated in patients with a history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions e.g., toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) or Stevens-Johnson syndrome] to its active ingredients (nirmatrelvir or ritonavir) or any other components of the product."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0010",\n                "section": "2.4 Use in Patients with Hepatic Impairment",\n                "supporting_quote": "No pharmacokinetic or safety data are available regarding the use of nirmatrelvir or ritonavir in subjects with severe (Child-Pugh Class C) hepatic impairment; therefore, PAXLOVID is not recommended for use in patients with severe hepatic impairment ."\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "contraindicated_populations": [\n                "Patients with history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions to active ingredients or components",\n                "Patients taking contraindicated drugs (primarily CYP3A-metabolized or strong CYP3A inducers)",\n                "Patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class C) (not recommended)"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "000ecd1d6df50389",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "The recommended dosage regimen of Paxlovid is: 1) Standard dosage for patients with normal renal function or mild renal impairment (eGFR \\u226560 mL/min): 300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) taken together orally twice daily (morning and bedtime) for 5 days. 2) Patients with moderate renal impairment (eGFR \\u226530 to <60 mL/min): 150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) taken together orally twice daily for 5 days. 3) Patients with severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min), including those requiring hemodialysis: Day 1: 300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once. Days 2-5: 150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once daily. On dialysis days, the dose should be administered after hemodialysis. The 5-day treatment course should be initiated as soon as possible after diagnosis of COVID-19, and within 5 days of symptom onset.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0004",\n                "section": "2.2 Recommended Dosage",\n                "supporting_quote": "The recommended dosage for PAXLOVID is 300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) with all 3 tablets taken together orally twice daily in the morning and at bedtime for 5 days."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0007",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage in Patients with Renal Impairment",\n                "supporting_quote": "Days 1-5 150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) twice daily Severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min) including those requiring hemodialysis On days of hemodialysis, the PAXLOVID dose should be administered after hemodialysis."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0008",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage in Patients with Renal Impairment",\n                "supporting_quote": "Day 1 300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0009",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage in Patients with Renal Impairment",\n                "supporting_quote": "Days 2-5 150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once daily"\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "standard_dosage": "300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) orally twice daily for 5 days",\n            "moderate_renal_impairment_dosage": "150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) orally twice daily for 5 days",\n            "severe_renal_impairment_dosage": {\n                "day_1": "300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once",\n                "days_2_5": "150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once daily"\n            },\n            "treatment_initiation": "As soon as possible after diagnosis of COVID-19 and within 5 days of symptom onset"\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "b54c25890c1ae3a6",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "Paxlovid should be administered orally, with or without food, at approximately the same time each day. All tablets in a dose (nirmatrelvir and ritonavir) must be taken together. The tablets must be swallowed whole and not chewed, broken, or crushed. If a dose is missed within 8 hours of the usual time, the patient should take it as soon as possible and resume the normal schedule. If missed by more than 8 hours, the patient should skip the missed dose and take the next dose at the regularly scheduled time (do not double the dose to make up for a missed dose). The full 5-day course must be completed.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0003",\n                "section": "2.1 Important Dosage and Administration Information",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID (both nirmatrelvir and ritonavir tablets) can be taken with or without food . The tablets should be swallowed whole and not chewed, broken, or crushed."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0176",\n                "section": "17 PATIENT COUNSELING INFORMATION",\n                "supporting_quote": "Advise patients to swallow all tablets for PAXLOVID whole and not to chew, break, or crush the tablets."\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "route": "oral",\n            "food_coadministration": "with or without food",\n            "tablet_instructions": "swallow whole, do not chew, break, or crush",\n            "missed_dose_instructions": {\n                "within_8_hours": "take as soon as possible and resume normal schedule",\n                "more_than_8_hours": "skip missed dose and take next dose at regular time"\n            }\n        }\n    }\n]\n\noutput = {"answers": answers}\nwith open(\'/workspace/answers.json\', \'w\') as f:\n    json.dump(output, f, indent=2)\nprint("Done generating answers.json")\nEOF\npython3 generate_answers.py\n'] min_timeout_sec: 1.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
