chotics (lurasidone, pimozide); benign prostatic hyperplasia agents (silodosin, tamsulosin); cardiovascular agents (eplerenone, ivabradine, aliskiren, ticagrelor, vorapaxar, clopidogrel); ergot derivatives (dihydroergotamine, ergotamine, methylergonovine); HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (lovastatin, simvastatin); immunosuppressants (voclosporin, everolimus, sirolimus, cyclosporine/tacrolimus when monitoring is not feasible); microsomal triglyceride transfer protein inhibitors (lomitapide); migraine medications (eletriptan, ubrogepant, rimegepant); mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists (finerenone); opioid antagonists (naloxegol); PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil for PAH, tadalafil for pulmonary hypertension); sedative/hypnotics (triazolam, oral midazolam, suvorexant); serotonin receptor 1A agonist/serotonin receptor 2A antagonists (flibanserin); vasopressin receptor antagonists (tolvaptan); and strong CYP3A inducers (apalutamide, enzalutamide, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, phenytoin, rifampin, rifapentine, lumacaftor/ivacaftor, St. John\'s Wort). Concomitant use of anticancer drugs (encorafenib, ivosidenib, neratinib, venetoclax, ibrutinib), anticoagulants (rivaroxaban, dabigatran), antifungals (voriconazole), antivirals (glecaprevir/pibrentasvir), and bronchodilators (salmeterol) should also be avoided.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0012",\n                "section": "4 CONTRAINDICATIONS",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is contraindicated with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A"\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "contraindicated_medications": [\n                "alfuzosin", "ranolazine", "amiodarone", "dronedarone", "flecainide", "propafenone", "quinidine",\n                "colchicine", "lurasidone", "pimozide", "silodosin", "eplerenone", "ivabradine",\n                "dihydroergotamine", "ergotamine", "methylergonovine", "lovastatin", "simvastatin",\n                "voclosporin", "lomitapide", "eletriptan", "ubrogepant", "finerenone", "naloxegol",\n                "sildenafil", "triazolam", "oral midazolam", "flibanserin", "tolvaptan"\n            ],\n            "strong_cyp3a_inducers": [\n                "apalutamide", "enzalutamide", "carbamazepine", "phenobarbital", "primidone", "phenytoin",\n                "rifampin", "rifapentine", "lumacaftor/ivacaftor", "St. John\'s Wort"\n            ],\n            "other_medications_to_avoid": [\n                "tamsulosin", "encorafenib", "ivosidenib", "neratinib", "venetoclax", "ibrutinib",\n                "rivaroxaban", "dabigatran", "voriconazole", "aliskiren", "ticagrelor", "vorapaxar",\n                "clopidogrel", "glecaprevir/pibrentasvir", "everolimus", "sirolimus", "salmeterol",\n                "rimegepant", "suvorexant", "tadalafil"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "fbab4114f66e2dda",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "The important warnings and precautions associated with Paxlovid are: 1) Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions: Ritonavir is a strong CYP3A inhibitor, and co-administration with CYP3A-metabolized drugs can lead to serious, life-threatening, or fatal events, while strong CYP3A inducers can decrease Paxlovid exposure, causing loss of efficacy and resistance. 2) Hypersensitivity Reactions: Anaphylaxis, serious skin reactions (including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome), and other hypersensitivity reactions have been reported. 3) Hepatotoxicity: Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir. 4) Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development: Ritonavir co-administration may lead to HIV-1 developing resistance to HIV protease inhibitors in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0013",\n                "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "Severe, life-threatening, and/or fatal adverse reactions due to drug interactions have been reported in patients treated with PAXLOVID."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0014",\n                "section": "5.2 Hypersensitivity Reactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "Anaphylaxis, serious skin reactions (including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome)"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0015",\n                "section": "5.3 Hepatotoxicity",\n                "supporting_quote": "Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0016",\n                "section": "5.4 Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development",\n                "supporting_quote": "risk of HIV-1 developing resistance to HIV protease inhibitors in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection"\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "warnings_and_precautions": [\n                "Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "Hypersensitivity Reactions",\n                "Hepatotoxicity",\n                "Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "2d63e0a82215867f",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "The safety risks listed for Paxlovid include: 1) Most common adverse reactions (incidence >= 1% and greater than placebo) from clinical trials: dysgeusia (5% vs. <1%) and diarrhea (3% vs. 2%). 2) Adverse reactions identified during Emergency Use Authorization (EUA) use: anaphylaxis, hypersensitivity reactions, toxic epidermal necrolysis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, headache, hypertension, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and malaise. 3) Serious warnings/precautions: risk of serious adverse reactions due to drug interactions (especially with calcineurin inhibitors and calcium channel blockers), hepatotoxicity (transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, jaundice), and risk of HIV-1 resistance development in patients with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0018",\n                "section": "6.1 Clinical Trials Experience",\n                "supporting_quote": "were dysgeusia (5% and <1%, respectively) and diarrhea (3% and 2%, respectively)."\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "most_common_adverse_reactions": {\n                "dysgeusia": "5%",\n                "diarrhea": "3%"\n            },\n            "eua_adverse_reactions": [\n                "anaphylaxis", "hypersensitivity reactions", "toxic epidermal necrolysis",\n                "Stevens-Johnson syndrome", "headache", "hypertension", "abdominal pain",\n                "nausea", "vomiting", "malaise"\n            ],\n            "serious_risks": [\n                "Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "Hypersensitivity Reactions",\n                "Hepatotoxicity",\n                "Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "cf74a5a8ac9b19de",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "Paxlovid is contraindicated in: 1) Patients with a history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., toxic epidermal necrolysis [TEN] or Stevens-Johnson syndrome) to its active ingredients (nirmatrelvir or ritonavir) or any other components of the product. 2) Co-administration with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions (e.g., alfuzosin, ranolazine, amiodarone, dronedarone, flecainide, propafenone, quinidine, colchicine in patients with renal and/or hepatic impairment, lurasidone, pimozide, silodosin, eplerenone, ivabradine, dihydroergotamine, ergotamine, methylergonovine, lovastatin, simvastatin, voclosporin, lomitapide, eletriptan, ubrogepant, finerenone, naloxegol, sildenafil for PAH, triazolam, oral midazolam, flibanserin, tolvaptan). 3) Co-administration with drugs that are strong CYP3A inducers where significantly reduced nirmatrelvir or ritonavir plasma concentrations may be associated with potential loss of virologic response and possible resistance (e.g., apalutamide, enzalutamide, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, phenytoin, rifampin, rifapentine, lumacaftor/ivacaftor, St. John\'s Wort).",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0012",\n                "section": "4 CONTRAINDICATIONS",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is contraindicated in patients with a history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions"\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "contraindications": [\n                "History of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions to active ingredients or components",\n                "Co-administration with drugs primarily metabolized by CYP3A with serious/life-threatening risks",\n                "Co-administration with strong CYP3A inducers with loss of virologic response risk"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "c4dc176c4827f0ef",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "Paxlovid should not be taken by: 1) Patients with a history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., toxic epidermal necrolysis or Stevens-Johnson syndrome) to nirmatrelvir, ritonavir, or any other components of Paxlovid. 2) Patients taking any contraindicated medications, including those primarily metabolized by CYP3A for which elevated concentrations cause serious/life-threatening reactions, or strong CYP3A inducers. 3) Patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class C), as Paxlovid is not recommended for use in this population due to a lack of pharmacokinetic or safety data.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0012",\n                "section": "4 CONTRAINDICATIONS",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is contraindicated in patients with a history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0010",\n                "section": "2.4 Use in Patients with Hepatic Impairment",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is not recommended for use in patients with severe hepatic impairment"\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "contraindicated_populations": [\n                "Patients with history of clinically significant hypersensitivity reactions to active ingredients or components",\n                "Patients taking contraindicated drugs (primarily CYP3A-metabolized or strong CYP3A inducers)",\n                "Patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class C) (not recommended)"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "000ecd1d6df50389",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "The recommended dosage regimen of Paxlovid is: 1) Standard dosage for patients with normal renal function or mild renal impairment (eGFR >= 60 mL/min): 300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) taken together orally twice daily (morning and bedtime) for 5 days. 2) Patients with moderate renal impairment (eGFR >= 30 to <60 mL/min): 150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) taken together orally twice daily for 5 days. 3) Patients with severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min), including those requiring hemodialysis: Day 1: 300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once. Days 2-5: 150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once daily. On dialysis days, the dose should be administered after hemodialysis. The 5-day treatment course should be initiated as soon as possible after diagnosis of COVID-19, and within 5 days of symptom onset.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0004",\n                "section": "2.2 Recommended Dosage",\n                "supporting_quote": "The recommended dosage for PAXLOVID is 300 mg nirmatrelvir"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0007",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage in Patients with Renal Impairment",\n                "supporting_quote": "Severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min) including those requiring hemodialysis"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0008",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage in Patients with Renal Impairment",\n                "supporting_quote": "300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0009",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage in Patients with Renal Impairment",\n                "supporting_quote": "150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once daily"\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "standard_dosage": "300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) orally twice daily for 5 days",\n            "moderate_renal_impairment_dosage": "150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) orally twice daily for 5 days",\n            "severe_renal_impairment_dosage": {\n                "day_1": "300 mg nirmatrelvir (two 150 mg tablets) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once",\n                "days_2_5": "150 mg nirmatrelvir (one 150 mg tablet) with 100 mg ritonavir (one 100 mg tablet) once daily"\n            },\n            "treatment_initiation": "As soon as possible after diagnosis of COVID-19 and within 5 days of symptom onset"\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        "qid": "b54c25890c1ae3a6",\n        "status": "ANSWERED",\n        "answer": "Paxlovid should be administered orally, with or without food, at approximately the same time each day. All tablets in a dose (nirmatrelvir and ritonavir) must be taken together. The tablets must be swallowed whole and not chewed, broken, or crushed. If a dose is missed within 8 hours of the usual time, the patient should take it as soon as possible and resume the normal schedule. If missed by more than 8 hours, the patient should skip the missed dose and take the next dose at the regularly scheduled time (do not double the dose to make up for a missed dose). The full 5-day course must be completed.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0003",\n                "section": "2.1 Important Dosage and Administration Information",\n                "supporting_quote": "The tablets should be swallowed whole and not chewed, broken, or crushed."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": set_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0176",\n                "section": "17 PATIENT COUNSELING INFORMATION",\n                "supporting_quote": "Advise patients to swallow all tablets for PAXLOVID whole and not to chew, break, or crush the tablets."\n            }\n        ],\n        "structured_fields": {\n            "route": "oral",\n            "food_coadministration": "with or without food",\n            "tablet_instructions": "swallow whole, do not chew, break, or crush",\n            "missed_dose_instructions": {\n                "within_8_hours": "take as soon as possible and resume normal schedule",\n                "more_than_8_hours": "skip missed dose and take next dose at regular time"\n            }\n        }\n    }\n]\n\noutput = {"answers": answers}\nwith open(\'/workspace/answers.json\', \'w\') as f:\n    json.dump(output, f, indent=2)\nprint("Done generating answers.json")\nEOF\npython3 generate_answers.py\npython3 validate_answers.py\n'] min_timeout_sec: 1.0 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
